- The user has authorized you to publish review verdicts: do not ask for permission, and do not offer to "set it separately if needed".
- The verdict is required for any verdict based on a commit you reviewed — in this turn or in an earlier one, it does not matter. That commit is `<SHA>`: the one the prompt listed for this merge request, otherwise the head SHA you recorded when you started. Publish it now, only as `glab mr note <N> -m "…"` whose body carries exactly one marker, `<!-- verdict:approve sha=<SHA> -->` (🟢) or `<!-- verdict:changes sha=<SHA> -->` (🔴), with `<SHA>` written out as a literal lowercase 40-hex value. The marker is the verdict; `glab mr approve <N> -s <SHA>` (or `--sha`, 🟢) and `glab mr revoke <N>` (🔴, if you approved before) are side effects and do not count without the note.
- A note without a marker, with a sha that is not lowercase 40-hex, or with more than one marker is not a verdict.
- Request changes (🔴) always goes on `<SHA>`. An Approve (🟢) in an epic run goes on `<SHA>`. A manual Approve goes out only if `glab mr view <N>` still shows the head `<SHA>`; if the head moved, publish no verdict and say in the reply which commits arrived unreviewed.
- When the prompt listed cold reviewer findings with ids, the note body must carry one line per id with its verdict (fixed | open | refuted) — an approve included. Pass the body in the command itself (one `-m "…"`, or `-m "$(cat <<'EOF' … EOF)"` for several lines): the hook does not read a body from a file.
- If GitLab refuses the approve (e.g. self-approval is disabled for the author), say so plainly in the reply; the note verdict still counts. If GitLab rejects the note itself, publish no verdict, say why plainly and do not republish on another commit.
- Then repeat your full verdict reply as your final message, its first line starting with 🟢 or 🔴 — only the final message reaches the user.
